A||N||3||lews84||Dumb Question: What does RTV stand for?||||5
Z||000000||lews84||10-03-1999||12:06 AM||||In my questions about fixing a leaky valve cover many (all?) reference RTV gasket sealer. What does RTV stand for?<P>Back in my old Formula Ford days, anything that had a gasket (and many things that didn't like the Hewland transaxle) were sealed with GE Silcone Seal. Any comments or comparisons between GE SS and RTV?<P>Thanks in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||10-03-1999||12:20 AM||||RTV= ROOM TEMPERATURE VULCANIZING. Meaning its a rubber based product that cures without heat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-03-1999||06:24 PM||||Well in this case... It's silicon based.<P>RTV & Silicon are used interchangably in the car world.<P>Where you get diferances in is the SI formulation. Some are made for specific applications. some are more generally usable.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||10-04-1999||12:49 AM||||Yeah,the thinner consistency of the(plain) normal variety is just fine;goes into small scratches,and wets out better than the new formulations(some called super blue/copper/whatever),so I use that kind,even though it's easier to spread the other kind in a thick layer.||||reg||1
